WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.373
Entity Framework Core is a powerful data access layer, but it is not a complete security boundary by itself.

2
00:00:07.373 --> 00:00:15.361
It can help developers express queries, map entities, track changes, and use safer provider-supported query patterns.

3
00:00:15.361 --> 00:00:25.397
It does not automatically decide who may read a record, which tenant owns it, which fields can be changed, or whether a workflow should be allowed.

4
00:00:25.397 --> 00:00:33.248
EF Core reduces some query-construction risk when developers stay inside supported LINQ and parameterized patterns.

5
00:00:33.248 --> 00:00:47.448
That is valuable, but secure data access still requires authorization, input validation, data shaping, least privilege, safe logging, careful migrations, dependency maintenance, and review of high-risk paths.

6
00:00:47.448 --> 00:00:50.657
The framework is a tool inside a larger design.

7
00:00:50.657 --> 00:00:54.548
Trust boundaries appear throughout the EF Core lifecycle.

8
00:00:54.548 --> 00:01:07.998
Controllers, APIs, background jobs, domain services, repositories, DbContext instances, database providers, administrators, reporting tools, and migration processes each make different assumptions.

9
00:01:07.998 --> 00:01:18.511
When data or identity crosses those boundaries, the application should be clear about what is trusted, what is validated, and what rule is being enforced.

10
00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:23.700
A safe framework default is not the same thing as a safe application design.

11
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.796
A query may be parameterized and still return too much data.

12
00:01:27.796 --> 00:01:32.780
A tracked entity may be valid and still allow an unintended field change.

13
00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:39.812
A migration may be technically correct and still introduce a risky permission or destructive operation.

14
00:01:39.812 --> 00:01:43.567
Security review has to look at intent, not only syntax.

15
00:01:43.567 --> 00:01:46.502
Secure EF Core usage is normal engineering.

16
00:01:46.502 --> 00:01:52.715
Teams should design data access that is understandable, reviewable, testable, and operable.

17
00:01:52.715 --> 00:01:55.582
The goal is not to avoid EF Core features.

18
00:01:55.582 --> 00:02:07.188
The goal is to use them deliberately so database behavior supports the same access rules, privacy expectations, and operational safeguards as the rest of the application.
